      "text": "To solve this problem, we will use the principle of inclusion-exclusion. Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Determine the total number of ways to color the hexagons without any restrictions:**\n   Each of the 12 hexagons can be either red, yellow, or green. Thus, there are \\(3^{12}\\) ways to color the hexagons without any restrictions.\n\n2. **Apply the Principle of Inclusion-Exclusion to subtract the cases where at least two adjacent hexagons are the same color:**\n   We need to subtract the cases where at least two adjacent hexagons are the same color. Let's denote the colors as \\(R\\), \\(Y\\), and \\(G\\).\n\n   - **Case 1: All hexagons of one color.**\n     There are 3 choices (all red, all yellow, or all green), but one of these choices is not valid because it would violate the rule that no two adjacent hexagons are the same color. Hence, there are \\(3 - 1 = 2\\) valid choices for each color.\n   - **For two adjacent hexagons the same color, there are 2 choices (either two red, two yellow, or two green).**\n   - **For three adjacent hexagons the same color, there are 1 choice (for three hexagons the same color).**\n\n   Using the principle of inclusion-exclusion:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Invalid cases} = 3 \\times (2 + 2 + 1) - 3 \\times 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 3 \\times 5 - 3 \\times 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 15 - 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 12\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the number of valid colorings:**\n   \\[\n   3^{12} - 12\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 531441 - 12\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 531429\n   \\]\n\nThus, the number of different ways to color the figure such that no two hexagons with a common side are the same color is \\(\\boxed{531429}\\).",
